narrative:
## One Function, Many Types

A **generic** is a function (or type) that works with ANY type. Instead of saying 'this works with Int,' you say 'this works with T — whatever T turns out to be.'

### Without Generics: Three Functions

```xiom
fn print_int_vec(v: Vec[Int]) {
  for item in v { io.println(item); }
}

fn print_str_vec(v: Vec[Str]) {
  for item in v { io.println(item); }
}

fn print_bool_vec(v: Vec[Bool]) {
  for item in v { io.println(item); }
}
```

These three functions are identical except for the type name. That's code duplication.

### With Generics: One Function

```xiom
fn print_vec[T](v: Vec[T]) {
  for item in v { io.println(item); }
}
```

The `[T]` after the function name declares a type parameter. `T` stands for 'any type.' When you call `print_vec(ints)`, `T` becomes `Int`. When you call `print_vec(strs)`, `T` becomes `Str`.

### Full Program

```xiom
fn print_vec[T](v: Vec[T]) {
  for item in v { io.println(item); }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let ints: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  ints.push(1); ints.push(2); ints.push(3);

  let strs: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  strs.push("a"); strs.push("b");

  print_vec(ints);
  print_vec(strs);
  return 0;
}
```

One `print_vec` function works for both `Vec[Int]` AND `Vec[Str]` — and any other type you throw at it.

